---
/** D2.2.5 genome, transcriptome and proteome compared in three human cell types. */
const genes = ['insulin', 'β-globin', 'albumin', 'ATP synthase'];
const cols = [
  { x: 130, name: 'Pancreatic β cell', on: [0, 3], amounts: [5, 2] },
  { x: 305, name: 'Developing red blood cell', on: [1, 3], amounts: [5, 2] },
  { x: 480, name: 'Liver cell', on: [2, 3], amounts: [5, 2] },
];
const W = 165;
const rows = [
  { y: 52, h: 96, name: 'Genome', sub: 'all genes' },
  { y: 156, h: 84, name: 'Transcriptome', sub: 'mRNAs made' },
  { y: 248, h: 92, name: 'Proteome', sub: 'proteins present' },
];
---
<svg viewBox="0 0 660 384" role="img" aria-labelledby="d22o-t d22o-d" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <title id="d22o-t">Genome, transcriptome and proteome of three cell types</title>
  <desc id="d22o-d">Three human cells: a pancreatic beta cell, a developing red blood cell and a liver cell. All three have the same genome, including the genes for insulin, beta-globin, albumin and ATP synthase. Each transcribes only some genes: the beta cell makes insulin and ATP synthase mRNA, the developing red blood cell beta-globin and ATP synthase mRNA, the liver cell albumin and ATP synthase mRNA. Their proteomes differ in the same way, and protein amounts are not simply proportional to mRNA amounts.</desc>
  {rows.map((r) => (
    <g>
      <rect class="row" x="6" y={r.y} width="648" height={r.h} rx="8" />
      <text class="rname" x="16" y={r.y + r.h / 2 - 4}>{r.name}</text>
      <text class="rsub" x="16" y={r.y + r.h / 2 + 14}>{r.sub}</text>
    </g>
  ))}
  {cols.map((c) => (
    <g>
      <text class="cname" x={c.x + W / 2} y="38" text-anchor="middle">{c.name}</text>
      {genes.map((g, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class="chip" x={c.x + (i % 2) * 84} y={60 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 30} width="80" height="24" rx="4" />
          <text class="glbl" x={c.x + (i % 2) * 84 + 40} y={76 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 30} text-anchor="middle">{g}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      <text class="small" x={c.x + W / 2} y="136" text-anchor="middle">+ all other genes</text>
      {c.on.map((gi, k) => (
        <g>
          <path class="mrna" d={`M${c.x + 6} ${180 + k * 30} q8 -6 16 0 t16 0`} />
          <text class="glbl" x={c.x + 46} y={184 + k * 30}>{genes[gi]} mRNA</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      {c.on.map((gi, k) => (
        <g>
          {Array.from({ length: c.amounts[k]! }, (_, j) => <circle class={`p ${gi === 3 ? 'p2' : 'p1'}`} cx={c.x + 12 + j * 16} cy={272 + k * 34} r="7" />)}
          <text class="glbl" x={c.x + 92} y={276 + k * 34}>{genes[gi]}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
    </g>
  ))}
  <text class="note" x="330" y="358" text-anchor="middle">Same genome in every cell, but different genes are transcribed, so the proteins differ.</text>
  <text class="note" x="330" y="374" text-anchor="middle">Protein amounts are not simply proportional to mRNA amounts.</text>
</svg>
<style>
  svg { font-family: var(--font-sans); }
  .row { fill: var(--surface); stroke: var(--border); }
  .rname { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 800; fill: var(--text); }
  .rsub { font-size: 12px; fill: var(--text-muted); }
  .cname { font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; fill: var(--text); }
  .chip { fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bio-head) 18%, var(--surface)); stroke: var(--bio-head); }
  .glbl { font-size: 12px; fill: var(--text); }
  .small { font-size: 12px; fill: var(--text-muted); font-style: italic; }
  .mrna { stroke: var(--bio-k); stroke-width: 3; fill: none; }
  .p1 { fill: var(--bio-protein); }
  .p2 { fill: var(--bio-protein-2); }
  .note { font-size: 12px; fill: var(--text-muted); font-style: italic; }
</style>
